    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="17" relname="concession">Everything about usability is subjective!</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="3" relname="circumstance">But when a vast majority ofusers can figure out an interface with little or no instruction,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="17" relname="span">thenit's considered intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="24" relname="volitional-cause">Actually, since intuitiveness is so closely tied to learnability,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="24" relname="span">Ithink the premise discussed in the Preece text would apply (p16):</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="25" relname="contrast">products intended for use by the general population, used everyday orinfrequently, must be easy to learn (intuitive),</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="25" relname="contrast">while products that arecomplex by design and have much more functionality require more time andeffort to learn.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="19" relname="antithesis">We talk about the 'intuitiveness' of Word, or some other complexapplication,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="19" relname="span">but we weren't born knowing what a "File Save" or "Paste"icon means.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="11" relname="nonvolitional-cause">These things have evolved to make certain things moreconsistent in complex applications</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="21" relname="span">and, hence, make other applicationsadhering to these principles seem more intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="13" relname="condition">If a free-thinker came up with a better icon for "Save" or "Paste",</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="22" relname="span">then I feel sure that it would be rejected</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="22" relname="nonvolitional-cause">because it is inconsistent withcurrent established conventions.</segment>
        <group id="15" type="span"/>
        <group id="16" type="span" parent="15" relname="span"/>
        <group id="17" type="span" parent="27" relname="span"/>
        <group id="18" type="span" parent="16" relname="span"/>
        <group id="19" type="span" parent="26" relname="span"/>
